Aether-Hollow

Ingredients: INDIE FOLK & FREAKFOLK + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Woodwinds  
Creative Catalyst: The genre apes the call-and-response of container ships greeting each other in fog.

Aether-Hollow sounds like a sea-cave recording of a lost space-shuttle crew playing acoustic instruments into a infinite reverb unit. Fingerpicked gut-string guitars and erratic, un-tempered wooden flutes drift over low-frequency, sub-bass drones that mimic the deep, reverberating hull-groans of distant maritime traffic. Long, microtonal woodwind swells act as foghorns, calling into vast, empty acoustic spaces only to be answered seconds later by pitched-shifted, tape-delayed responses from across the stereo field. It marries the unpolished, uncanny intimacy of early-2000s freakfolk with the crushing, weightless scale of orbital ambient soundscapes.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 52–68 BPM. The pulse is loose, breathing, and non-metered, driven by hand-tapped instrument bodies and low-end air-displacement rather than traditional percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Open-tuned acoustic guitars (DADGAD and C-G-D-G-B-D), Lydian modes, microtonal woodwind bends, and unresolved minor-ninth suspensions that simulate shifting atmospheric pressure.
- Production Signatures: Extreme distance-mic placement mixed with close-miked breath sounds; dynamic convolution reverb modeled on empty cargo holds and granite quarries; spatial pitch-shifting tape loops that decay into static.
- Dynamics: Glacial and cavernous; track arrangements sit in near-total silence before exploding into wide, wall-of-air swells of overlapping woodwinds and resonant resonance.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the winter of 2011 along the rocky, mist-drowned coast of the Bay of Fundy, Aether-Hollow was pioneered by a isolated collective of art-school dropouts, ex-mariners, and sound artists operating out of abandoned fish-processing plants. Wearing oversized, salt-encrusted woolen sweaters, vintage deep-sea diving thermals, and copper-wired wooden talismans, the scene gathered at low tide to perform. They tuned their bass flutes and wooden recorders to the exact resonant frequencies of the bulk carriers idling in the bay’s deep water channels. Performances were non-hierarchical circles where players would wait up to twenty seconds for an echo to return from across the harbor before laying down the next chord, treating the natural acoustics of coastal fog and iron hulls as an active bandmember.

Key Instrumentation
- Woodwinds (Central): Contrabass Flute, Wooden Bass Recorder, Irish Low Whistle, and C-Melody Saxophone played through tape delays.
- Strings & Acoustic: Open-tuned 12-string acoustic guitar, pre-war parlor guitar, bowed banjo, and zither.
- Atmospherics & Synths: EMS Synthi VCS3 (for solar wind noise), sub-harmonic bass generators, and re-spooled cassette Dictaphones.

