Track 2: Kattegat-Skagerrak
- Story & Context: Named after the turbulent strait where Vance’s grandfather worked as a line-thrower, this track serves as an unvarnished confession of spatial isolation written during a three-week isolation in a disused grain silo. The music relies on a haunting call-and-response between a bowed 12-string guitar and a microtonal alto recorder that mimics Scandinavian ship-to-shore distress codes. The recording process was interrupted when a local foghorn drowned out the middle verse, a mistake the band kept because it harmonized perfectly with the guitar’s open D string. Over the droning tide of woodwinds, Vance delivers the track’s central hook: "Call me by the harbor name, not the name my mother gave."
